# RiftRace — Project Rules

## Product
RiftRace is a League of Legends challenge platform.

Authenticated users can:
- create an account with email, username and password;
- alternatively sign in with Google;
- create challenges;
- configure and manage their own challenges;
- add participants;
- view challenge progress and rankings.

A challenge has exactly one type:
- SOLOQ: up to 16 individual players.
- DUOQ: up to 8 duos, therefore 16 players maximum.

The challenge creator is the owner of the challenge.

## Challenge lifecycle
A challenge has a configured start date and time.

Before the configured start:
- display that the challenge has not started;
- do not count post-start challenge statistics;
- clearly display the scheduled start date/time.

After the start:
- display participant progression;
- display LP gained;
- display rank comparison;
- display the information visually and intuitively.

The product should favor fast comprehension over dense tables.

## Riot data refresh
A challenge can be manually refreshed at most once every 2 minutes.

The UI must clearly indicate:
- whether refresh is available;
- when the next refresh will be available;
- that data may not be real-time.

Do not implement a client-side workaround that bypasses this limit.

The backend is responsible for enforcing the refresh limit. The frontend indicator is only UX.

## Architecture
Use a clear separation:
- Angular frontend;
- Spring Boot backend;
- PostgreSQL database;
- Riot API accessed only by the backend.

Never expose Riot API credentials to the browser.

## Engineering principles
- Prefer simple, maintainable solutions.
- Do not over-engineer the MVP.
- Keep business logic out of Angular components.
- Keep Riot API integration isolated behind backend services/clients.
- Validate all user input on the backend.
- Never trust values sent by the frontend.
- Use DTOs between API and domain/persistence layers where appropriate.
- Use database migrations; never rely on manually created production tables.
- Do not introduce a library without a concrete reason.
- Preserve existing architecture unless a change is justified.
- Do not make unrelated refactors while implementing a feature.

## Security
Never commit:
- passwords;
- password hashes;
- Riot API keys;
- OAuth client secrets;
- database credentials;
- JWT signing secrets;
- production environment files containing secrets.

Passwords must be stored using a modern password hashing algorithm. Never store plaintext passwords.

## Time
Store timestamps in UTC in the backend/database.
Convert to the user's locale only for presentation.
The challenge start date/time must be unambiguous.

## Workflow with Cursor
Before implementing a large feature:
1. Inspect the existing code and relevant rules.
2. Explain the implementation plan briefly.
3. Identify affected files.
4. Implement the smallest coherent change.
5. Add/update tests.
6. Report what changed and any assumptions.

Do not rewrite the application wholesale unless explicitly requested.
